  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 1227 PM CDT MON AUG 3 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery late morning reveals an upper trough\nstretching from the Great Lakes region down towards the Gulf\ncoast. This trough will leave a cutoff low across the region as\nthe main trough lifts off to the northeast. Moisture wrapping\naround the low will lead to low to medium chances for showers and\nstorms daily, generally across our eastern areas. By Thursday,\nthe low will begin to drift north, eventually being absorbed by\nanother approaching upper level trough moving across the Ohio\nValley. A COL pattern sets up over the weekend, leading to\ndiurnally driven showers and thunderstorms into early next week.\n\nToday and tomorrow will be our \"coolest\" days of the week with\nhighs in the mid 80s to around 90 degrees. We start to warm back\nup by mid week with highs rebounding into the low to mid 90s.\nHumid conditions will keep max heat indices in the 95-100 degree\nrange which will pose a moderate heat risk for much of Central\nAlabama.\n\nOtherwise, there is a low (20-30%) chance for patchy fog early\nTuesday morning. Latest RAP13 guidance hints at pockets of\nnarrowing dew points and condensation pressure deficits from 1-\n3mb, mainly east of I-65. Those that see shower/storm activity\ntoday will be more likely to see fog development.\n\n95/Castillo",
